cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "THEIA: grounded radiogenomic prediction of EGFR status from lung CT"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Loke
    given-names: Sathvik
repository-code: "https://github.com/sathvikloke/THEIA"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  A vision-language model that localises lung tumours from CT while attempting to
  predict EGFR mutation status. The result that holds is the localisation:
  attention supervised against tumour segmentations transfers to 420 held-out
  NSCLC-Radiomics patients from a different country and scanner fleet, with an
  attention-mass lift of +0.297 over a per-fold spatial shuffle, beating a centre
  prior on the pointing game (0.688 vs 0.476) and a randomly-initialised head
  (+0.000). The genotype prediction does not hold. On NSCLC-Radiogenomics (153
  labelled patients, 40 EGFR-mutant) it reaches a pooled out-of-fold AUC of
  0.618 +/- 0.025, against 0.794 for smoking status alone; adding it on top of
  five clinical variables changes AUC by -0.029 +/- 0.015 with every seed's
  confidence interval including zero; a logistic regression on frozen features
  matches it exactly; and its probabilities score worse on Brier than predicting
  the base rate for every patient. An independent 1,646-patient cohort reports
  the same ceiling. See docs/RESULTS.md.
keywords:
  - radiogenomics
  - non-small cell lung cancer
  - EGFR
  - weakly supervised localisation
  - reproducibility
